---
import '../styles/timing-block.css';
---

<div class="mt-box">
  <h3>How Morse Code Timing Works</h3>
  <p>
    Morse code has no letters or sounds of its own. It is built from short and long signals, and everything is measured
    against one unit of time: the length of a dot.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-table-wrap">
    <table class="mt-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Part</th>
          <th scope="col">Length</th>
          <th scope="col">Example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="mt-r1">
          <td data-label="Part"><span class="mt-chip">Dot</span></td>
          <td data-label="Length"><span class="mt-part">1 unit</span></td>
          <td data-label="Example"><span class="mt-code">.</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="mt-r2">
          <td data-label="Part"><span class="mt-chip">Dash</span></td>
          <td data-label="Length"><span class="mt-part">3 units</span></td>
          <td data-label="Example"><span class="mt-code">-</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="mt-r3">
          <td data-label="Part"><span class="mt-chip">Gap inside a letter</span></td>
          <td data-label="Length"><span class="mt-part">1 unit</span></td>
          <td data-label="Example">Between the dot and dash in A (<span class="mt-code">.-</span>)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="mt-r4">
          <td data-label="Part"><span class="mt-chip">Gap between letters</span></td>
          <td data-label="Length"><span class="mt-part">3 units</span></td>
          <td data-label="Example">Between A and B</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="mt-r5">
          <td data-label="Part"><span class="mt-chip">Gap between words</span></td>
          <td data-label="Length"><span class="mt-part">7 units</span></td>
          <td data-label="Example">Between HELLO and WORLD</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Using this table, the letter <strong>A</strong> (<span class="mt-code">.-</span>) takes 5 units: one for the dot, one
    for the gap, and three for the dash.
  </p>

  <h3>How speed is measured</h3>
  <p>
    Morse speed is counted in <strong>words per minute (WPM)</strong>. Because words have different lengths, operators
    use one test word, <strong>PARIS</strong>. It takes exactly 50 units to send, including the pause after it. To find
    how long one dot lasts, divide 1,200 by your speed.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-cards">
    <div class="mt-card a">
      <div class="big">10 WPM</div>
      <div class="sub">Dot = 120 ms · Dash = 360 ms</div>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-card b">
      <div class="big">20 WPM</div>
      <div class="sub">Dot = 60 ms · Dash = 180 ms</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>Beginners usually start slow and speed up as their ear improves.</p>

  <h3>International and American Morse</h3>
  <p>
    The code used today is <strong>International Morse code</strong>, the standard set by the ITU (ITU-R M.1677). An
    older version, <strong>American Morse code</strong>, was used on U.S. telegraph lines and has some different letters
    and spacing.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-note">
    <strong>Quick tip:</strong> You do not need to calculate any of this by hand. Press play in the translator and listen
    to the rhythm instead.
  </div>
</div>
